  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nBy Tuesday evening, the remnants of TC Polo are progged to have \nlargely phased with the broader upper low, resulting in a plume of \nsignificant and anomalous moisture flowing over NM and adjacent West \nTX with PWATs approaching 2\" (nearly 4 standard deviations above \nnormal) in spots. Heavy showers and thunderstorms are expected to \nincrease in coverage especially after 00z Tuesday as an upper level \nvort max associated with the remnants of Polo results in a notable \nincrease in large scale forcing for ascent. The surface and low \nlevel response will also be fairly significant with most models \ndepicting a sub-1000mb surface low developing over SE NM which will \nresult in strong sustained winds over the Caprock Tuesday night. The \ncurrent expectation is that one or more broken lines of \nthunderstorms will move over our area Tuesday evening through the \novernight hours into early Wednesday, with the highest coverage of \nstorms occurring to the west of I-27. \n\nA few severe storms are possible Tuesday evening, with forecast \nsoundings depicting strong flow up to the tropopause and surface-\nbased CAPE exceeding 1000 J/kg despite an elongated profile. This \ncombined with 30-40kt of SSW flow in the 850-700mb layer will \nsupport the potential for isolated severe wind gusts, in addition \nto a nonzero tornado threat over portions of the South Plains \ngiven the presence of backed surface flow. However, the primary \nhazard associated with this event is the potential for numerous \ninstances of flash flooding given training storms and highly \nefficient rainfall. Hi-res ensemble suites still favor areas \nalong and west of a line from Tulia to Littlefield to Morton for \nthe heaviest rainfall totals and highest risk of flash flooding, \nand a Flood Watch remains in effect for the SW Panhandle and NW \nSouth Plains. Areas in the Flood Watch generally have a 50-70% \nchance of rainfall totals exceeding 2\" through Wednesday morning. \nSwaths of 3-4\" are also not out of the question, though totals of \nthis magnitude will be more common to our west and north over NM \nand towards I-40. Rainfall totals are expected to decrease fairly \nquickly with eastward extent, with some locations east of I-27 \npotentially only seeing a half inch or less.\n\nThe bulk of the heavy precipitation is expected to end by Wednesday \nmorning as a dry slot punches eastward on the southern end of the \nupper low. Although some lingering scattered showers are a good bet \nthrough much of Wednesday, additional rainfall totals will only be \nlight. Another window for severe storms will then open Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, this time across the Rolling Plains along and \neast of a dryline. This threat should concentrate mainly to our \neast, but a few instances of large hail and damaging winds could \nmaterialize on Wednesday over our eastern zones. For the rest of the \nweek, the forecast generally trends drier and cooler. There will be \nsome potential for a few showers late week mainly off the Caprock, \nbut our current expectation is for most locations to remain dry \nThursday and beyond. The more noticable change will be much cooler \ntemperatures with highs currently forecast in the 60s and 70s \nThursday and Friday.",
